#pragma once

#include "/Plugin/CloudAtmosphere/Private/GasGiantConstants.ush"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Grid, topology and operators for the gas giant flow sim.
//
// THE GRID IS A CYLINDER, NOT A TORUS.
//
// Longitude wraps: column 0 and column N-1 are neighbours, period exactly 2 pi.
// Latitude does NOT wrap. The top and bottom rows are adjacent to the POLES,
// not to each other. Treating this as a torus joins the north pole to the south
// pole, and material advected out of the arctic arrives in the antarctic.
//
// Sampler state must therefore be WRAP on U and CLAMP on V. Nothing in this
// file uses a sampler -- every read is an explicit Load through SimWrapCoord,
// because the pole is a topological fold that no address mode expresses -- but
// the OUTPUT texture the material reads does use one, and it needs those modes.
//
// THE LATITUDE COORDINATE IS MU = sin(latitude), NOT LATITUDE.
//
// Three separate things fall out of this and any one of them would justify it:
//
//   The metric collapses. The spherical Laplacian in (lambda, mu) is
//   d/dmu[(1-mu^2) d/dmu] + 1/(1-mu^2) d2/dlambda2, with two closed-form
//   coefficients and no cross terms. In latitude it carries 1/cos(phi) factors
//   that have to be seen to cancel rather than being absent.
//
//   Cells are equal AREA. Every texel covers 2 pi dmu / N_lon of surface,
//   identically, everywhere. So an area-weighted mean is a plain mean, a zonal
//   mean is a plain row mean, and the global mean is a plain mean of row means.
//   Those three reductions are the most-used quantities in the whole model.
//
//   The material's V coordinate is free: V = 0.5 + 0.5 * N.z, no asin. U still
//   costs an atan2, and that is the only transcendental in the fetch.
//
// TEXEL CENTRES SIT BETWEEN THE POLES, NOT ON THEM.
//
// Cell-centred, so mu runs over [-1 + dmu/2, 1 - dmu/2] and the poles are cell
// FACES. This is not a detail. The Laplacian's latitude coefficient is
// (1 - mu^2) evaluated at the face, which is EXACTLY ZERO at the pole face, so
// the flux through the pole vanishes by construction and the operator needs no
// polar boundary condition at all -- no ghost rows, no lambda -> lambda + pi
// reflection, no special case in the solver.
//
// A vertex-centred grid puts texels ON the poles, where 1/(1-mu^2) is infinite
// and a whole row of texels represents one physical point. Every finite
// difference code that does that then spends its polar boundary condition
// undoing the damage. Half a texel of offset removes the problem instead.
//
// The fold survives in exactly one place: INTERPOLATION. A departure point can
// legitimately land past the pole, and crossing it means lambda -> lambda + pi.
// SimWrapCoord is the only function that knows this.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/** Grid dimensions. x = longitude columns, y = latitude rows, z = layer count.
 *  Longitude should be even; the polar fold assumes N_lon/2 is an integer
 *  column offset, and an odd width lands the reflection half a texel off. */
int3 SimGridSize;

/** float3(1/N_lon, 1/N_lat, unused). */
float3 SimInvGridSize;

// -- Coordinates ------------------------------------------------------------

/** Longitude of column i, in radians, at the texel CENTRE. */
float SimColumnToLon(float I)
{
    return (I + 0.5f) * SimInvGridSize.x * (2.0f * PI);
}

/** mu = sin(latitude) of row j, at the texel CENTRE. Never reaches +-1. */
float SimRowToMu(float J)
{
    return -1.0f + 2.0f * (J + 0.5f) * SimInvGridSize.y;
}

/** mu at the FACE between row J-1 and row J. Reaches exactly -1 at J = 0 and
 *  exactly +1 at J = N_lat, which is what zeroes the polar flux. */
float SimFaceToMu(float J)
{
    return -1.0f + 2.0f * J * SimInvGridSize.y;
}

/** Unit direction from grid coordinates. Right-handed, Z along the rotation
 *  axis, matching the material's planet-local space. */
float3 SimDirection(float Lon, float Mu)
{
	// cos(latitude). Positive by construction since |mu| < 1 strictly on a
	// cell-centred grid, so no max() guard is needed and none is written -- a
	// guard here would hide a grid setup error rather than fix one.
    const float C = sqrt(1.0f - Mu * Mu);

    float SinL, CosL;
    sincos(Lon, SinL, CosL);

    return float3(C * CosL, C * SinL, Mu);
}

// NOTE: the trig-based tangent basis helpers that used to live here are gone.
// SimTangentVelocity builds the same vectors from the direction with no trig,
// and nothing else needed them once the velocity texture began storing east and
// north COMPONENTS rather than a 3D vector. Leaving them would have been a
// second way to build the same basis, which is the shape of mistake that put
// the streamfunction into the advection in the first place.

/** Continuous grid coordinates from a unit direction. The inverse of
 *  SimDirection, in texel-index space so that integer values land on centres.
 *
 *  This is the only atan2 in the sim, and it runs once per texel per advection
 *  step rather than per pixel. */
float2 SimDirectionToGrid(float3 N)
{
    float Lon = atan2(N.y, N.x);

	// atan2 returns (-pi, pi]; the grid runs [0, 2 pi).
    Lon = (Lon < 0.0f) ? Lon + 2.0f * PI : Lon;

    const float I = Lon * (1.0f / (2.0f * PI)) * SimGridSize.x - 0.5f;
    const float J = (N.z + 1.0f) * 0.5f * SimGridSize.y - 0.5f;

    return float2(I, J);
}

// -- Topology ---------------------------------------------------------------

/** Fold an arbitrary integer texel coordinate onto the grid.
 *
 *  THE ONE PLACE THE SPHERE'S TOPOLOGY LIVES.
 *
 *  Longitude is a plain periodic wrap. Latitude is a MIRROR with a half-turn in
 *  longitude, because stepping north past the pole comes back down the far
 *  side: row -1 is row 0 at longitude + pi, row -2 is row 1 at longitude + pi,
 *  and so on. Written as a mirror rather than a clamp because a clamp would
 *  duplicate the polar row and give the interpolator a flat spot exactly where
 *  the flow crosses the pole, which reads as material piling up at the pole and
 *  is easy to mistake for a convergence artifact in the solver.
 *
 *  The half-turn is why N_lon must be even.
 *
 *  Applied AFTER the mirror for longitude, since the mirror changes it. */
int2 SimWrapCoord(int2 C)
{
    const int NLon = SimGridSize.x;
    const int NLat = SimGridSize.y;

	// Latitude mirror across whichever pole was crossed. Written so that
	// multiple crossings are impossible in practice -- a departure point cannot
	// travel more than a fraction of the grid in one step -- but a single
	// crossing is handled exactly.
    if (C.y < 0)
    {
        C.y = -1 - C.y;
        C.x += NLon / 2;
    }
    else if (C.y >= NLat)
    {
        C.y = 2 * NLat - 1 - C.y;
        C.x += NLon / 2;
    }

	// Periodic longitude. Two-step modulo so negative inputs land correctly;
	// HLSL % keeps the sign of the dividend.
    C.x = ((C.x % NLon) + NLon) % NLon;

    return C;
}

// -- Sampling ---------------------------------------------------------------

/** Point read of a scalar field with full topology handling. */
float SimLoad(Texture2DArray<float> Field, int2 C, uint Layer)
{
    const int2 W = SimWrapCoord(C);

    return Field.Load(int4(W.x, W.y, Layer, 0));
}

/** Catmull-Rom weights for one axis. */
void SimCubicWeights(float F, out float W0, out float W1, out float W2, out float W3)
{
    const float F2 = F * F;
    const float F3 = F2 * F;

    W0 = 0.5f * (-F3 + 2.0f * F2 - F);
    W1 = 0.5f * (3.0f * F3 - 5.0f * F2 + 2.0f);
    W2 = 0.5f * (-3.0f * F3 + 4.0f * F2 + F);
    W3 = 0.5f * (F3 - F2);
}

/** Bicubic (Catmull-Rom) interpolation at continuous grid coordinates.
 *
 *  BILINEAR HERE WOULD KILL THE INVERSE CASCADE, and it would do it in a way
 *  that looks like the physics failing rather than the interpolation failing.
 *
 *  Semi-Lagrangian advection is unconditionally stable and NOT conservative:
 *  its error is a numerical diffusion whose magnitude is set by the
 *  interpolator. Bilinear diffuses at roughly the grid scale per step, and the
 *  2D inverse cascade -- the entire mechanism that makes eddies grow into
 *  structure rather than dissipating -- is an inviscid property. Smear the
 *  small scales and the cascade has nothing to feed on, so eddies vanish, the
 *  field relaxes to pure zonal flow within a couple hundred steps, and the
 *  result is a planet with perfect bands and no weather.
 *
 *  That failure looks exactly like "the seeding didn't take" or "the forcing is
 *  too weak", so it is worth knowing that it is neither.
 *
 *  Sixteen taps, all through SimWrapCoord, so this is correct across the
 *  longitude seam and across the poles. Hardware bilinear cannot be used for
 *  the same reason: no address mode expresses the polar fold.
 *
 *  Catmull-Rom overshoots at sharp gradients, which for vorticity is
 *  acceptable and even desirable -- it is the anti-diffusion that keeps
 *  filaments sharp. It is NOT acceptable for a quantity that must stay
 *  positive; nothing advected here is. */
float SimSampleCubic(Texture2DArray<float> Field, float2 Grid, uint Layer)
{
	// INDEX SPACE, NOT UV SPACE. Texel centres sit on INTEGERS here, because
	// that is what SimDirectionToGrid produces: column i maps to exactly i.
	//
	// The half-integer form -- floor(G - 0.5) + 0.5 -- is the correct base for
	// UV*Size coordinates, where centres sit at 0.5, 1.5, 2.5. Using it on
	// index-space input samples at G - 0.5 instead of G, in BOTH axes, for
	// every input value.
	//
	// That failure is worth naming because it does not look like an
	// interpolation bug. A constant offset in the departure point is
	// indistinguishable from a constant velocity added to the flow: the field
	// drifts steadily in +longitude and +mu at half a texel per step regardless
	// of what the fluid is doing. At 512x256 and a 0.02 step that is 0.31 rad
	// per unit time zonally -- three times the jet speed -- and a poleward
	// sweep that crosses the sphere in about ten time units.
	//
	// So it reads as "the sim has a mysterious current in it", and because the
	// two axes drift by an equal number of TEXELS rather than an equal arc,
	// it reads as a stronger current in latitude than in longitude.
    const float2 Base = floor(Grid);
    const float2 F = Grid - Base;

	// Catmull-Rom taps run from one before the containing texel to two after,
	// and the weights below put full weight on tap 1 when F is zero. So tap 1
	// must be floor(Grid), which makes the first tap floor(Grid) - 1.
    const int2 C0 = (int2) Base - int2(1, 1);

    float WX0, WX1, WX2, WX3;
    SimCubicWeights(F.x, WX0, WX1, WX2, WX3);

    float WY0, WY1, WY2, WY3;
    SimCubicWeights(F.y, WY0, WY1, WY2, WY3);

    const float WX[4] = { WX0, WX1, WX2, WX3 };
    const float WY[4] = { WY0, WY1, WY2, WY3 };

    float Sum = 0.0f;

	[unroll]
    for (int J = 0; J < 4; J++)
    {
		[unroll]
        for (int I = 0; I < 4; I++)
        {
            Sum += WX[I] * WY[J] * SimLoad(Field, C0 + int2(I, J), Layer);
        }
    }

    return Sum;
}

// -- Operators --------------------------------------------------------------

/** The five-point spherical Laplacian in (lambda, mu), FLUX FORM.
 *
 *  Flux form rather than expanded form, and this is the whole reason the polar
 *  boundary condition disappears. Writing
 *
 *      d/dmu[ (1-mu^2) dpsi/dmu ]  ~  [ A+ (psi_j+1 - psi_j) - A- (psi_j - psi_j-1) ] / dmu^2
 *
 *  with A+- evaluated at the cell FACES means the coefficient at the polar face
 *  is (1 - (+-1)^2) = 0 exactly, in floating point, with no epsilon. The flux
 *  through the pole is identically zero because the geometry says it is, and no
 *  ghost row is ever consulted. The expanded form -- (1-mu^2) psi'' - 2 mu psi'
 *  -- evaluates its coefficients at cell centres instead, where they are not
 *  zero, and then needs a polar condition invented to compensate.
 *
 *  The longitude coefficient 1/(1-mu^2) does still grow toward the poles, which
 *  is the residual pole problem and is what the zonal filter in the forcing
 *  pass exists to bound. It is a conditioning problem, not a correctness one.
 *
 *  Returns the operator applied to Field at C, and also the DIAGONAL
 *  coefficient, because the solver needs both and recomputing the coefficients
 *  separately is how the two drift apart. */
void SimLaplacianCoeffs(int Row, out float CLon, out float ALo, out float AHi)
{
    const float DLon = (2.0f * PI) * SimInvGridSize.x;
    const float DMu = 2.0f * SimInvGridSize.y;

    const float Mu = SimRowToMu((float) Row);

	// Faces bounding this row. At Row == 0 the lower face is mu = -1 exactly
	// and at Row == N_lat-1 the upper face is mu = +1 exactly, so ALo or AHi is
	// exactly zero there and the mirrored neighbour is multiplied out.
    const float MuLo = SimFaceToMu((float) Row);
    const float MuHi = SimFaceToMu((float) (Row + 1));

    ALo = (1.0f - MuLo * MuLo) / (DMu * DMu);
    AHi = (1.0f - MuHi * MuHi) / (DMu * DMu);

	// 1/(1-mu^2) is finite on a cell-centred grid: the closest mu gets to +-1
	// is one half texel away. At 256 rows that is a coefficient of about 1.6e4
	// against an interior value of 1, which is stiff but bounded.
    CLon = 1.0f / ((1.0f - Mu * Mu) * DLon * DLon);
}

/** The operator, given its five taps.
 *
 *  Factored this way for one reason: the SOR sweep reads the streamfunction
 *  back through its own UAV rather than through an SRV, because red-black
 *  ordering means no thread reads a texel that another thread in the same
 *  dispatch writes, and binding the same resource twice to dodge that would be
 *  a hazard for no gain. HLSL has no way to write one function over both
 *  Texture2DArray and RWTexture2DArray, so the alternative was two copies of
 *  the coefficient math -- which is precisely the kind of duplicate that gets
 *  edited once and stays wrong in the other copy for weeks.
 *
 *  The taps differ between call sites. The operator does not. */
float SimLaplacianFromTaps(
	int Row,
	float Centre,
	float West,
	float East,
	float South,
	float North,
	out float OutDiagonal)
{
    float CLon, ALo, AHi;
    SimLaplacianCoeffs(Row, CLon, ALo, AHi);

    OutDiagonal = -(2.0f * CLon + ALo + AHi);

    return CLon * (West - 2.0f * Centre + East)
		+ AHi * (North - Centre)
		- ALo * (Centre - South);
}

float SimLaplacian(Texture2DArray<float> Field, int2 C, uint Layer, out float OutDiagonal)
{
    return SimLaplacianFromTaps(
		C.y,
		SimLoad(Field, C, Layer),
		SimLoad(Field, C + int2(-1, 0), Layer),
		SimLoad(Field, C + int2(1, 0), Layer),
		SimLoad(Field, C + int2(0, -1), Layer),
		SimLoad(Field, C + int2(0, 1), Layer),
		OutDiagonal);
}

// NOTE: the velocity helper that used to live here has been removed rather than
// left unused. MainVelocityCS now differences the POLAR-MASKED streamfunction,
// and a second entry point that differenced the raw one would be an easy
// mistake to make and a hard one to see -- it would work everywhere except
// within a few degrees of the poles.

/** Rebuilds a 3D tangent velocity from its stored east/north components.
 *
 *  The velocity texture stores TWO components, not three, because a tangent
 *  vector on a sphere has two degrees of freedom and the freed channel is worth
 *  more elsewhere. Anything reading that texture has to reconstruct, and this
 *  is the one place that knows how.
 *
 *  NO TRIG. With c = length(N.xy) the basis is east = (-N.y, N.x, 0)/c and
 *  north = (-N.z*N.x/c, -N.z*N.y/c, c), which are the vectors sincos(longitude)
 *  would give, obtained from N directly.
 *
 *  Identical to GG_FlowVelocity in the material. The two must agree, because
 *  the sim advects along this and the material warps along it -- a mismatch
 *  would put the clouds on a different flow from the one that made them. */
float3 SimTangentVelocity(float3 N, float2 Components)
{
    const float C = max(length(N.xy), 1e-5f);

    const float3 East = float3(-N.y, N.x, 0.0f) / C;
    const float3 North = float3(-N.z * N.x / C, -N.z * N.y / C, C);

    return East * Components.x + North * Components.y;
}

/** Half-width, in texels, of the longitudinal box filter at this latitude.
 *
 *  THE POLE PROBLEM, AND WHY THIS IS A FILTER RATHER THAN A SEPARATE CAP GRID.
 *
 *  Physical longitudinal spacing is cos(phi) * dlambda, which goes to zero at
 *  the poles. Two consequences, both fatal if untreated: the advective CFL
 *  condition tightens without bound so the polar rows go unstable at a timestep
 *  the rest of the grid is comfortable with, and the Laplacian's 1/(1-mu^2)
 *  coefficient makes the Poisson operator stiff enough that the solver stops
 *  converging there.
 *
 *  A running mean over 1/cos(phi) texels removes exactly the longitudinal
 *  wavenumbers that are over-resolved -- the ones the grid carries but the
 *  physical spacing cannot support -- and leaves everything else. It restores a
 *  uniform effective resolution in physical distance, which is what a reduced
 *  grid does, without the bookkeeping of an actually reduced grid.
 *
 *  The alternative considered and rejected was a separate polar cap grid on its
 *  own projection. That is a second solver with a second metric and an overlap
 *  region reconciled every step, and reconciliation error at an overlap shows
 *  up as a persistent ring at a fixed latitude -- a structural artifact that
 *  looks like a real atmospheric feature and is therefore easy to ship by
 *  accident. This is twenty lines and its failure mode is visible blur.
 *
 *  What it costs is polar detail, and Jupiter's poles genuinely do have detail:
 *  Juno found cyclone clusters there. Those are discrete, long-lived, and would
 *  not emerge from a solve at this resolution regardless, so they belong on the
 *  authored-vortex path rather than being something the sim is asked for. */
int SimFilterHalfWidth(float Mu, float FilterLatitude, int MaxHalfWidth)
{
    const float CosPhi = sqrt(1.0f - Mu * Mu);

	// Above the threshold latitude nothing is filtered at all, so the tropics
	// and mid-latitudes -- where all the visible structure is -- are untouched.
    if (CosPhi >= FilterLatitude)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    const float Width = FilterLatitude / max(CosPhi, 1e-4f);

    return clamp((int) round((Width - 1.0f) * 0.5f), 0, MaxHalfWidth);
}
